==English==

===Etymology===
{{suffix|en|froth|y}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-frothy.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# [[foamy|Foamy]] or [[churn]]ed to the point of becoming [[infuse]]d with [[bubble]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1903| title=Report on the Results of Investigations Into Cidermaking|author=Frederick James Lloyd | page=50| passage=When this brown head was obtained the juice was drawn off into barrels, the lees were left in the keeve, and new juice was pumped upon these lees, this, however, produced a white '''frothy''' head, similar to that in the remaining keeves.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1964| title=A Photographic Study of Boiling Flow |author=John Henry Vohr | page=113| passage=The motion of this liquid layer was made visible by the small , '''frothy''' bubbles of vapor which were present in the film.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992| title=Gourmet - Volume 52, Issues 1-6 |editor=Pearl Violette Metzelthin | page=128| passage=For a '''frothy''' result, in a blender blend the hot chocolate in batches.}}
#: {{ux|en|I like my milkshakes '''frothy''', not flat like this!}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} [[lightweight]]; lacking depth or substance
#: {{ux|en|songs with '''frothy''' lyrics}}
#: {{ux|en|a '''frothy''' argument}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1825| title=The lady of the manor|author=Mary Martha Sherwood | page=87| passage=I cannot describe to you what pain I feel in repeating this light and '''frothy''' conversation, but I have compelled myself to give it you at some length, in order to lay before you, in its true light, the weakness of that heart which is not upheld by divine strength, and to shew you how little dependence can be placed on those who walk in their own strength, and are not divinely upheld.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1827|author={{w|Robert Pollok}}|title=The Course of Time
|passage=The '''frothy''' orator, who busked his tales <br>In quackish pomp of noisy words}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1921| title=Parliamentary Debates: House of Representatives|author=New Zealand Parliament | page=888| passage=Now, I ask those honourable gentlemen who have given us a good deal of '''frothy''' argument this evening, without much business basis in it, who is going to pay that bill?}}
# {{lb|en|business}} Highly [[speculative]]; having high risk and high return.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1974 | title=Parliamentary Papers |author=Australia. Parliament | page=297| passage=But we have found it profitable to engage in some trading on our own account, generally in the rather '''frothy''' areas.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010| title=What's Wrong with Social Policy and How to Fix It |author=Bill Jordan | page=47| passage=First, many of the welfare gains of the previous two decades or more turned out to have been illusory, as house and share prices tumbled, jobs disappeared and '''frothy''' investment opportunities collapsed. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013| title=Alternative Investments|author=H. Kent Baker, Greg Filbeck | page=| passage=In such a “'''frothy'''” market, investors' interests would generally have been served by selling rather than purchasing assets—and, in fact, by returning committed capital to investors rather than deploying it, since acquisitions generally would not be accretive.}}

